window.docContents[110].resolve({"/Design-restrictions/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions":{"contents":"The objective of Core C++ is a well behaved C++. Informally, a language is well behaved when its specification leaves no case underspecified. The subset keeps one representative construct per category, the expressions, commands, abstraction, encapsulation, type systems and library of the earlier chapters.\n\nEach syntactic class, such as the expressions or the commands, has its own judgement. The rules of a judgement state the cases they cover, and every other case of its class is error. The specification achieves this pattern without error rules, .\n\nCore C++ is a subset of C++17, and every Core C++ program compiles with g++ -std=c++17. Each restriction of the subset removes a construct, or a freedom that C++17 leaves to the compiler. For each restriction, this chapter states what C++17 allows, what Core C++ admits and the property of the semantics the restriction gives.\n\nThe properties are those the earlier chapters state. Every construct has a typing rule and an evaluation rule. A program has at most one result, . A well typed program has a derivation, does not terminate, or reaches a case that the evaluator reports as error, . Lean proves the first statement. The second has no Lean proof yet, so it is a property checked by inspection of the rule set.\n\nThe behaviour of C++ in this chapter comes from N4659, the final working draft of C++17, open-std.org. It also comes from g++ -std=c++17, which on the machine of these tests is Apple clang 21.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Core C++","header":"9. Design restrictions","id":"/Design-restrictions/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions"}});